## Why This Is Asked

Assesses design of policy-driven mutating webhooks, dynamic CRD-backed configuration, and production-grade reliability (fail-closed, caching, hot reload, observability).

## Key Concepts

- MutatingAdmissionWebhook lifecycle, failure modes, and timeouts
- Policy CRD design with namespace overrides and defaults
- In-memory policy cache and Kubernetes watch-based invalidation
- Observability: metrics (latency, patch count), tracing, and audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
function applyPolicyToPod(pod, policy) {
  const ns = pod.metadata.namespace;
  const nsPolicy = policy[ns] || policy["default"];
  if (!nsPolicy) return pod;

  const containers = (pod.spec.containers || []).map((c) => {
    const req = c.resources?.requests || {};
    const lim = c.resources?.limits || {};
    const newReq = { ...req, cpu: nsPolicy.maxCPU, memory: nsPolicy.maxMemory };
    const newLim = { ...lim, cpu: nsPolicy.maxCPU, memory: nsPolicy.maxMemory };
    return { ...c, resources: { requests: newReq, limits: newLim } };
  });
  return { ...pod, spec: { ...pod.spec, containers } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you version policies and handle breaking changes?
- How would you test webhook fail-closed semantics under policy backend latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a production-grade control plane feature that enforces resource fairness, minimizes disruption, and scales across tenants and clusters.

## Key Concepts
- CRD-driven budgets per node and per-namespace priorities
- Reconciliation loop with breach detection, drain/re-schedule, and preemption safety
- Interaction with PodDisruptionBudget, CSI volumes, and QoS tiers
- Observability: metrics, alerts, audit trails, and tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo reconciler skeleton for NodeBudget controller
class NodeBudgetController {
  reconcile(nodeBudgetCR) {
    const node = getNode(nodeBudgetCR.spec.nodeName)
    const budget = nodeBudgetCR.spec.budget
    const usage = getNodeUsage(node)
    if (usage.cpu > budget.cpu || usage.mem > budget.mem) {
      const candidates = selectNonCriticalPods(node)
      drainPods(candidates)
      rescheduleToHealthyNodes(candidates)
      emitEventsAndMetrics(node, usage, budget)
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model priority and fairness when multiple nodes breach budgets simultaneously?
- How would you test this with a simulated workload and ensure no data loss during drains?
